  "client_view_text": " A write-ahead log is a file that contains a record of all changes made to the database. When a change is made to the database, the change is first written to the log, and then the change is written to the database itself. This ensures that the log always contains a record of all changes made to the database, even if the database itself is not updated.\nWhen a crash occurs, the database can use the log to recover the changes that were made before the crash. The database can read the log and apply the changes to the database, effectively undoing the effects of the crash. This process is called \"recovery\" or \"redoing\" the log. The database can also use the log to detect and correct any inconsistencies that may have occurred during the crash.\nThe write-ahead log provides a way for the database to recover from a crash by providing a record of all changes made to the database. This allows the database to recover the changes that were made before the crash and to correct any inconsistencies that may have occurred during the crash. This ensures that the database remains consistent and reliable, even in the event of a crash.\nThe write-ahead log is a critical component of a database's crash recovery mechanism. It provides a way for the database to recover from a crash"
